531:
No person shall engage in, conduct or carry on the business of media production on private or public property within the limits of the city without first applying for and receiving a media production permit from the city.
This permit is in addition to any permit that may be required by the International Fire Code or any other applicable code.
(a) This permit is allowed with conditions in all zoning districts. Whenever a permit is sought for activities in primarily residential neighborhoods, extra consideration shall be given to the conditions imposed to ameliorate as much as possible inconvenience to the residents.
(b) Two classifications of media production permits are allowed:
(1) Low-impact activities are generally those activities having an intended duration of no more than seven consecutive days, with little or no disruption to normal and customary use of the site and adjacent and nearby properties and uses; but excluding activities requiring more than 35 total personnel or 35 vehicles, or utilizing more than one property site. Two or more contiguous parcels with direct inter-parcel access may be considered as a single site; and
(2) High-impact activities are generally those activities having an intended duration of eight or more consecutive days, which do not comply with the low-impact standards of this division, and/or will disrupt normal and customary use of the site and adjacent and nearby properties and uses. Any media production activities involving automobile crashes or chases, gunfire, pyrotechnics, explosives, the use of aircraft, stunts, and similar activities are examples of high-impact activities.
(c) General standards. Prior to making application for a permit, it shall be incumbent on the applicant to meet with the city administrator to review locations and the general scope of activities planned during the media production. A copy of the script shall be made available, upon request, to allow the city staff to better understand the applicant's needs.
The following general standards shall apply to all permits:
(1) All temporary structures, including, but not limited to, tents, temporary sanitary facilities, trash collection, or food service facilities shall be located in accordance with requirements for accessory structures as set forth in chapter 78;
(2) Tents, depending on their square footage, and other temporary structures may require a separate building permit from the city or approval of the county fire marshal; any food preparation or food service provided at the location shall require a food service permit from the county health department, or evidence of exemption;
(3) Lighting for media production should be oriented away from adjacent or nearby properties as much as possible and shall not interfere with the safe movement of vehicular traffic;
(4) Temporary signage must be approved in writing, in advance, by the city administrator prior to erection; permanent signage shall be restored by the permittee following completion of the media production activity;
(5) No regulatory traffic control signage within a public right-of-way shall be removed or modified without the prior written consent of the applicable jurisdiction; for the City of Berkeley Lake, such consent must be granted by the city engineer;
(6) No modification to any existing traffic signage, street light, traffic control device or other officially-installed facility or improvement shall be made without prior consent of the applicable jurisdiction; for the City of Berkeley Lake, such consent must be granted by the city engineer;
(7) No street, road, alley, or sidewalk shall be closed, barricaded or other obstruction erected within a public right-of-way, and no access shall be denied or restricted to public or private property or facilities unless the closure or restriction is approved by the respective governing authority having jurisdiction over each affected road or infrastructure; reasonable alternative access shall be arranged whenever possible;
(8) No trees, vegetation (including grasses), or ornamental shrubs shall be cut or removed from public property without prior written consent of the city in the permit, based upon an agreed landscape restoration plan. A land disturbance permit may be separately required depending upon the nature and extent of the activity;
(9) Facilities and locations to be used for base camp, food service, and parking shall be approved as a condition of the permit; any other required permit(s) shall be obtained prior to issuance of the permit under this division;
(10) The City of Berkeley Lake shall be listed in the production credits for all permits required.
(Ord. No. O-174-14, § 1, 8-21-2014; Ord. No. O-201-17, § 1, 4-20-2017)

